  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how dryland soils shift from a healthy, biologically active state to desertification, with the condition of the surface biocrust at the center of the transition. On the left, intact biocrust and vegetation are associated with favorable soil functions: high fertility, strong water retention and stability, active carbon and nitrogen cycling, and high biological abundance and biodiversity. Arrows and labels for gases and nutrients such as CO2, N2, CH4, NH4+ and NO3− suggest active exchange between the atmosphere and soil.\n\nAcross the landscape, precipitation appears to decline as the surface becomes barer and more degraded. A central box highlights the main pressures driving this change, including temperature shifts, drought, over-farming, overgrazing, deforestation, and surface disturbance. As these stresses increase, the soil surface becomes cracked and sparse, and the right side shows the resulting degraded condition: erosion, alkalinization, reduced carbon and nitrogen cycling, lower fertility, and biodiversity loss.\n\nOverall, the figure presents desertification as a progressive breakdown of biocrust-supported ecosystem processes, linking climate and land-use pressures to visible soil degradation and declining ecosystem function.",
